{"id":"ltc-disclosure-related-parties","title":"Who the related parties are, and what the homes themselves took out for Medi-Cal","question":"Which companies under common ownership did these homes pay, for what, and how much did the homes' own reports adjust before counting the cost for Medi-Cal?","finding":"The 42 reports list $71.0 million of transactions with 32 related parties (names as written). The largest is PACS (25 homes, $31.6 million, support services). 10 lines are related-party building leases ($14.6 million), of which the reports themselves remove $11.7 million for Medi-Cal. Across all lines the reports' own adjustments net $-11.3 million: related-party prices are taken out and replaced by the related company's cost, including $40.9 million of home-office cost added on lines with no transaction amount.","how":"Page 10.4 (1) and (2) of each report: name of the related party, description, whether it also sells to non-related buyers (column 4), transaction amount (column 7) and amount of adjustment (column 9). Lines that point to 'see page 10.4 (1)' are given the property company named there when only one is named. Grouped by the party's name as written.","limits":"Medi-Cal rules count a related company at its own cost, not its price, so a removed related-party lease is replaced by the owner's actual property costs; a large removal is the rule working, not a penalty. A party that sells to outside buyers may be paid its market price. The adjustments say what Medi-Cal will recognise, not that anyone was overcharged. Names are as the homes typed them.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"party","t":"Related party (as written)","type":"org"},{"k":"group","t":"Group of the paying homes","type":"text"},{"k":"homes","t":"Homes","type":"int"},{"k":"amount","t":"Transactions","type":"usd"},{"k":"adj","t":"Adjustment in the reports","type":"usd"},{"k":"what","t":"For","type":"text"},{"k":"sellsOutside","t":"Also sells to others?","type":"text"},{"k":"where","t":"Part